WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.740
If one input depolarizes a cell, do two identical inputs depolarize it twice as

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:09.740
much?

3
00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:15.823
Take our cell. The leak is 10 nS, tied at minus −65 mV.

4
00:00:16.543 --> 00:00:17.843
Threshold is minus −50

5
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:20.847
All the values here are illustrative.

6
00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:24.827
The cell is passive, and its voltage is the same everywhere.

7
00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:30.067
Voltages above threshold are passive predictions, with the spike switched off.

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:36.290
Open a steady excitatory input of 5 nS, tied at 0 mV.

9
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:40.673
The passive prediction is near minus −43 mV.

10
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:45.253
That is about 22 mV of depolarization, above threshold.

11
00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:48.853
A real cell with this threshold would fire on the way.

12
00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:55.286
It gets there with a shorter time constant, about 13 ms, because the total

13
00:00:55.286 --> 00:00:56.286
conductance grew.

14
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.590
Now open a second, identical input at the same time.

15
00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.750
Does the depolarization double, to about 43 mV?

16
00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:15.993
No. The passive prediction is about −32.5 mV.

17
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:19.393
That is about one and a half times as much, not twice.

18
00:01:20.416 --> 00:01:22.796
To see why, measure the voltage from rest.

19
00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.356
Then the leak drops out of the average.

20
00:01:26.416 --> 00:01:29.596
The leak is tied at rest, so its top-line term is zero.

21
00:01:30.476 --> 00:01:33.516
But the leak is still in the bottom line, and still carries current.

22
00:01:34.566 --> 00:01:39.406
Each top-line term is a synapse's conductance, times how far its rope's end sits

23
00:01:39.406 --> 00:01:40.406
from rest.

24
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.590
The bottom line is still the total conductance.

25
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.380
Two inputs double the top line.

26
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.020
But they also enlarge the bottom line.

27
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:53.683
With one input, the top is 325, over 15.

28
00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:59.503
With two, the top doubles to 650, but the bottom also grows, to 20.

29
00:02:00.516 --> 00:02:02.456
So the second input dilutes the first.

30
00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:07.406
That is the same effect, seen as ropes.

31
00:02:08.226 --> 00:02:12.686
The marker is nearer 0 mV, so each AMPA rope's driving force has shrunk.

32
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.690
This is sublinear summation.

33
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.170
Inputs sharing one small patch of membrane show it most.

34
00:02:20.183 --> 00:02:24.503
Notice where the surprise lived: in the bottom line, the total conductance.

35
00:02:25.416 --> 00:02:28.376
Each new input adds to the total conductance.

36
00:02:28.936 --> 00:02:32.916
So the voltage settles at a weighted average, not a sum of millivolts.

37
00:02:33.836 --> 00:02:34.916
Small inputs nearly add.

38
00:02:35.996 --> 00:02:36.836
Large ones fall short.
